// Weekly couples studies — 52 entries, one per week, selected by week number.
const WEEKLY = [
{t:"Love Is Patient",ref:"1 Corinthians 13:4-7",intro:"Paul's famous description of love is less a wedding poem and more a mirror. Try reading it with your own name in place of the word 'love' — where the sentence turns false, that's this week's growth area. Real love is built from these small, repeatable behaviors.",q:["Read the passage with your name substituted for 'love.' Which line was hardest to say?","Which of these qualities first drew you to your spouse?","Where has your patience with each other grown since you married? Where has it thinned?","'Love keeps no record of wrongs' — do either of you keep a mental record? What's on it?","Which one phrase from this passage do you each want to work on this week?"],act:"Each of you pick one phrase (e.g., 'is not irritable') as your assignment. Check in Friday: how did it go, honestly?",prayer:"Father, You are patient and kind with us. Grow that same love between us this week."},
{t:"Two Are Better Than One",ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:9-12",intro:"Solomon says two are better than one because of the return on shared labor: when one falls, the other lifts them up. Marriage is God's design for not facing life alone — and the 'cord of three strands' hints that the strongest marriages have God as the third strand.",q:["When has your spouse 'lifted you up' after a fall — literal or figurative?","In what areas do you work well as a team? Where do you work like two solo acts?","What does the 'third strand' — God in your marriage — look like in practice right now?","Where is one of you carrying something alone that should be shared?","What's one 'good reward for labor' you've earned together that you should celebrate?"],act:"Pick one task or burden one of you has been carrying solo and do it together this week.",prayer:"Lord, be the third strand in our cord. Make us stronger together than apart."},
{t:"Leaving and Cleaving",ref:"Genesis 2:24-25",intro:"God's first word about marriage: leave, be joined, become one flesh. Leaving isn't just moving out of your parents' house — it's making your spouse the first human loyalty, above family of origin, friends, even your own independence. Cleaving is choosing each other again, daily.",q:["What did each of you have to 'leave' to fully join this marriage?","Is there any loyalty — family, friends, work, habits — that still competes with your spouse?","What does 'one flesh' mean beyond the physical?","'Naked and not ashamed' — where do you feel fully known and safe with each other? Where do you still hide?","How can you 'cleave' — actively choose each other — this week?"],act:"Tell each other one thing you've never quite said out loud — a hope, fear, or memory. Practice being fully known.",prayer:"Father, help us leave everything that competes, and cleave to each other without shame."},
{t:"Words That Build Up",ref:"Ephesians 4:29-32",intro:"Paul sets a filter for every sentence: no corrupt talk, only what builds up and gives grace to those who hear. In marriage, words compound — daily deposits of grace or daily leaks of contempt. Your spouse's heart is largely built out of your sentences.",q:["What words from your spouse this month built you up? Be specific.","What kind of talk tears you down fastest — criticism, sarcasm, silence?","Paul says our words can 'grieve the Holy Spirit.' How does that change how you think about tone at home?","What sentence do you most need to hear from your spouse regularly?","What sentence do you most need to retire from your vocabulary?"],act:"Every day this week, say one specific, out-loud affirmation to your spouse — no repeats.",prayer:"Lord, set a guard over our mouths. Let every word between us give grace."},
{t:"Quick to Listen",ref:"James 1:19-20",intro:"Swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to anger — James gives the sequence, and most marital arguments run it exactly backwards. Listening isn't waiting for your turn to talk; it's working to understand before working to be understood. Anger rarely produces the righteousness of God, but listening often produces peace.",q:["On a scale of 1–10, how well listened-to do you feel lately? What would move the number up?","What does your spouse do that makes you feel truly heard?","Which comes fastest for you under stress — speaking or anger? What triggers it?","What's one topic you've stopped bringing up because you didn't feel heard?","How can you signal 'I'm really listening' to each other this week?"],act:"Try a 10-minute listening exchange: one talks for 5 minutes uninterrupted, the other only reflects back what they heard. Then switch.",prayer:"Father, make us swift to hear each other, slow to speak, and slow to anger."},
{t:"Forgiving Each Other",ref:"Colossians 3:12-14",intro:"'Bear with one another and forgive each other... even as Christ forgave you.' Married forgiveness isn't a one-time event; it's the daily maintenance of two sinners sharing a roof. The standard is steep — as Christ forgave you — and the payoff is a home where failure isn't fatal.",q:["What small, unspoken resentments have collected lately? Name one gently.","What makes it hard for you to say 'I was wrong' — pride, fear, habit?","How did Christ forgive you? What would that look like between you two?","Is there an older wound between you that's never been fully forgiven?","What helps you feel truly forgiven — words, time, affection, changed behavior?"],act:"Each of you ask: 'Is there anything I've hurt you with that we've never resolved?' Listen without defending. Forgive out loud.",prayer:"Jesus, You forgave us everything. Help us keep short accounts and forgive each other fully."},
{t:"Serving One Another",ref:"John 13:12-15",intro:"The King of the universe knelt with a towel and washed feet — then said, 'I have given you an example.' In marriage, the question quietly shifts from 'Am I being served well?' to 'Whose feet am I washing?' Two servants make a great marriage; two customers make a miserable one.",q:["When has your spouse served you in a way that genuinely moved you?","What's the 'foot-washing' in your house — the humble task nobody wants?","Do you keep score of who does more? What does keeping score do to love?","What form of service speaks loudest to you — help, time, thoughtfulness?","Where have you been waiting to be served when you could serve first?"],act:"Secretly take over one of your spouse's most disliked chores this week. Don't announce it — let them notice.",prayer:"Jesus, You knelt to serve us. Give us both a towel and a servant's heart at home."},
{t:"Praying Together",ref:"Matthew 18:19-20",intro:"Jesus promises that where two agree and gather in His name, He is there among them. Couples who pray together regularly report some of the strongest marriages — not magic, but the effect of two people regularly humbling themselves before God in each other's hearing. It's hard to stay cold toward someone you hear praying for you.",q:["How often do you actually pray together, outside of meals? What gets in the way?","What feels awkward or vulnerable about praying out loud with each other?","What's one thing you've never asked your spouse to pray about for you?","When has a prayer you prayed together been answered?","What would a realistic, sustainable rhythm of praying together look like?"],act:"Pray together out loud every night this week, even if it's 60 seconds. Keep it simple: thanks, needs, each other.",prayer:"Lord, You are here among us. Build a rhythm of prayer into the core of our marriage."},
{t:"Sunset on Your Anger",ref:"Ephesians 4:26-27",intro:"'Be angry, and don't sin. Don't let the sun go down on your wrath, and don't give place to the devil.' Anger itself isn't the sin — buried, nursed, overnight anger is where the enemy gets a foothold. Paul's counsel is a deadline: deal with it before you sleep on it.",q:["How do you each handle anger — eruption, withdrawal, silence, sarcasm?","What happens in your marriage when anger goes unresolved overnight?","What's the difference between dropping an issue and actually resolving it?","What 'foothold' has unresolved conflict given the enemy in your home before?","What's your plan for the next conflict — a phrase, a pause, a rule?"],act:"Agree on a 'sundown rule' this week: before bed, ask 'Are we okay?' — and mean it. If not, talk until you are, or at least until you're on the same team again.",prayer:"Father, teach us to be angry without sinning, and never to sleep as enemies."},
{t:"Treasure and Trust",ref:"Matthew 6:19-21",intro:"'Where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.' Money fights are rarely about money — they're about security, control, dreams, and fear. Jesus reframes the whole subject: store treasure in heaven, and money becomes a tool you steward together instead of a master you serve separately.",q:["What did money mean in each of your childhood homes — security, stress, silence?","Where do your money instincts differ — spender/saver, risk/safety? How do you handle that?","What does your spending honestly say about where your treasure is?","Is there any money secret or worry you've been carrying alone?","What's one generous thing you could do together this month?"],act:"Have a no-blame money hour this week: review where things stand, pick one shared goal, and choose one act of generosity together.",prayer:"Lord, everything we have is Yours. Make us united and generous stewards."},
{t:"Rest Together",ref:"Mark 6:31",intro:"Jesus saw His disciples so busy they couldn't even eat, and prescribed rest: 'Come away by yourselves to a quiet place.' Exhausted people make poor spouses — short tempers, thin affection, parallel lives. Rest together isn't a luxury; it's maintenance for the one-flesh life.",q:["On a scale of 1–10, how rested are you? How rested is the marriage?","When did you last have unhurried, agenda-free time together?","What does real rest look like for each of you? (They may differ!)","What would you have to say 'no' to in order to rest together weekly?","What did you love doing together before life got this full?"],act:"Put a two-hour 'come away' block on the calendar this week — no phones, no chores, no logistics talk. Protect it like an appointment.",prayer:"Jesus, You invite us to rest. Help us stop striving long enough to enjoy You and each other."},
{t:"Grateful for Each Other",ref:"1 Thessalonians 5:16-18",intro:"'In everything give thanks' — including in marriage, where familiarity quietly erodes gratitude. The traits you fell for are still there; they've just become wallpaper. Gratitude is attention. This week is about seeing your spouse again, on purpose.",q:["What first attracted you to each other? Tell the story again.","What does your spouse do routinely that you've stopped thanking them for?","How does your spouse's presence make your life measurably better?","When did you last feel genuinely appreciated by your spouse?","Why is gratitude out loud so much more powerful than gratitude assumed?"],act:"Each day this week, thank your spouse for one specific thing you normally take for granted.",prayer:"Father, thank You for this exact person. Open our eyes to each other again."},
{t:"Loyal Love",ref:"Ruth 1:16-17",intro:"Ruth's vow — 'where you go, I will go... your people shall be my people, and your God my God' — is covenant loyalty in its purest form, spoken with nothing to gain. Marriage runs on this kind of hesed love: stubborn, chosen, all-weather commitment that outlasts feelings and circumstances.",q:["What has 'where you go, I will go' cost each of you so far?","When have you felt most certain of your spouse's loyalty?","What tests loyalty most in this season — distance, stress, temptation, exhaustion?","Ruth bound herself to Naomi's God too. How does shared faith deepen loyalty?","If you rewrote your vows today, knowing what you know, what would you add?"],act:"Write each other a two-sentence 'Ruth vow' for this season of life and read them aloud.",prayer:"God of covenant, make our loyalty to each other as stubborn as Yours to us."},
{t:"Christ at the Center",ref:"Colossians 3:17",intro:"'Whatever you do, in word or in deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus.' A Christ-centered marriage isn't two people staring at each other — it's two people walking toward the same Lord, finding themselves closer together with every step. When He's central, everything else finds its orbit.",q:["Honestly: what sits at the center of your marriage right now — kids, work, survival, Christ?","What does a Christ-centered decision look like versus a convenience-centered one?","Where have you seen God clearly at work in your marriage's history?","What spiritual practice as a couple has lapsed that you miss?","What's one 'word or deed' area to consciously do 'in His name' this week?"],act:"Pick one shared spiritual habit to restart this week — a devotion, a prayer walk, worship in the car. Small and consistent beats grand and abandoned.",prayer:"Jesus, take Your place at the center of this marriage. Reorder everything else around You."},
{t:"Humility at Home",ref:"Philippians 2:3-8",intro:"'In humility, count others better than yourselves' — then Paul points to Jesus, who emptied Himself. Nowhere is humility tested like at home, where being right feels so available. The strongest move in most marital standoffs is the humble one: going low first.",q:["Which is harder for you: apologizing first, or accepting an apology graciously?","Where does 'being right' cost you connection?","What would 'counting your spouse better than yourself' change in a typical disagreement?","How did Jesus 'going low' win everything? What might your version be?","Is there a standoff between you right now waiting for someone to go low first?"],act:"This week, whoever realizes they're in a standoff goes low first — softens, apologizes for their part, serves. Notice what it unlocks.",prayer:"Jesus, You went lowest of all. Give us the strength to go low first with each other."},
{t:"Truth in Love",ref:"Ephesians 4:15",intro:"'Speaking truth in love, we grow up.' Truth without love is a weapon; love without truth is a lullaby. Marriage needs both: the safety to say hard things and the tenderness to say them well. Couples who can't tell each other the truth slowly become strangers who share a house.",q:["Is there a truth you've been avoiding telling because of how it might land?","When your spouse gives you hard feedback, what makes it receivable?","Have you ever used truth as a weapon in an argument? What was the result?","What topics are 'off limits' between you that maybe shouldn't be?","How can you make it safer for each other to be honest?"],act:"Each of you share one gentle, true thing you've been sitting on — and receive your spouse's without defensiveness. Love first, truth wrapped inside.",prayer:"Father, make us truthful and tender at the same time, so we grow up together."},
{t:"The Soft Answer",ref:"Proverbs 15:1-4",intro:"'A gentle answer turns away wrath, but a harsh word stirs up anger.' Every escalating argument is built from matched harshness — and can be dismantled by one person refusing to match it. The soft answer isn't weakness; it's the strongest steering wheel in the house.",q:["Replay your last argument: where exactly did it escalate?","What tone or phrase from your spouse instantly raises your defenses?","What does a 'soft answer' sound like when you're genuinely angry?","Verse 4 says a gentle tongue is 'a tree of life.' What has your spouse's gentleness made grow in you?","What's your personal plan for softening your next heated moment?"],act:"Agree on a de-escalation signal — a word or gesture either of you can use that means 'let's lower the temperature, same team.' Use it at least once, even in a small moment.",prayer:"Lord, when anger rises between us, put a soft answer in our mouths."},
{t:"Bear One Another's Burdens",ref:"Galatians 6:2",intro:"'Bear one another's burdens, and so fulfill the law of Christ.' Your spouse carries weights you can't see unless you ask — pressures, fears, private battles. Burden-bearing starts with knowing what the burdens are. This week, trade inventories.",q:["What's the heaviest thing you're carrying right now? (Really answer.)","Does your spouse know? Why or why not?","How does your spouse best help you carry things — solutions, presence, prayer?","Is there a burden you've been dumping rather than sharing — or hiding rather than telling?","How does bearing each other's burdens 'fulfill the law of Christ'?"],act:"Take a walk together and each answer one question: 'What's heaviest for you right now?' Then each take one concrete piece of the other's load this week.",prayer:"Jesus, You carried what we couldn't. Teach us to carry each other's loads gladly."},
{t:"Spur One Another On",ref:"Hebrews 10:24-25",intro:"'Consider how to provoke one another to love and good works.' Notice the word consider — encouragement is a planned act, not an accident. Nobody on earth is better positioned to spur you toward God and your calling than your spouse. Are you each other's biggest fans or quietest critics?",q:["What dream or calling in your spouse deserves more of your encouragement?","When has your spouse's belief in you changed what you attempted?","What discourages your spouse fastest? What lifts them fastest?","How can you 'provoke' each other toward God without nagging?","Who else encourages your marriage? Do you need more of that community?"],act:"Identify one thing your spouse is trying to do — a habit, project, or calling — and actively fuel it this week: words, time, tangible help.",prayer:"Father, make us each other's greatest encouragers toward love and good works."},
{t:"Guard the Garden",ref:"Song of Solomon 2:15",intro:"'Catch for us the foxes, the little foxes that spoil the vineyards.' It's rarely the wolf at the door that damages a marriage — it's the little foxes: small neglects, drifting screens, secret irritations, slow busyness. Healthy couples fox-hunt regularly, naming small threats before they grow teeth.",q:["What 'little foxes' are nibbling at your marriage right now — name them honestly.","What boundaries protect your marriage — with technology, opposite-sex friendships, work hours?","What small neglect, left alone five more years, would do real damage?","What guards did you have early in marriage that have quietly relaxed?","What's one fox you can trap together this week?"],act:"Do a 20-minute 'fox hunt': each list three small things eroding closeness. Pick one each to eliminate this week.",prayer:"Lord, show us the little foxes and give us the diligence to catch them early."},
{t:"Honor and Faithfulness",ref:"Hebrews 13:4",intro:"'Let marriage be held in honor among all.' Faithfulness isn't just the absence of an affair — it's the daily practice of honoring the marriage in thought, word, and habit. We guard fiercely what we honor deeply. This week is about keeping the treasure treasured.",q:["What does 'honoring' your marriage look like in how you speak about each other publicly?","What situations, habits, or people make either of you feel less than fully secure?","What does faithfulness in thought life require of each of you?","How do you honor your marriage when you're apart — travel, work, online?","What's one way to visibly honor your marriage this month?"],act:"Each name one hedge you'll strengthen this week — a boundary, a habit, a transparency. Then plan something that publicly celebrates being married to each other.",prayer:"Father, keep us faithful in body, mind, and heart. Let our marriage be held in honor."},
{t:"This Is My Friend",ref:"Song of Solomon 5:16",intro:"The bride says of her husband: 'This is my beloved, and this is my friend.' Romance gets the poetry, but friendship carries the years. Couples who stay happily married for decades almost always describe each other the same way — best friends. Friendship, like any garden, needs tending.",q:["What made you friends before (or beneath) the romance?","What do you do together purely for fun? When did you last do it?","What does your spouse love talking about that you could enter into more?","If you weren't married, what would you admire about each other as friends?","What friendship habit — inside jokes, shared hobbies, long talks — needs reviving?"],act:"Do one purely-fun friend activity together this week — something with laughter and zero productivity.",prayer:"Father, deepen our friendship. Let us be able to say all our lives: this is my beloved, and this is my friend."},
{t:"Arise, My Love",ref:"Song of Solomon 2:10-13",intro:"'Rise up, my love, my beautiful one, and come away.' God devoted an entire book of the Bible to romantic love — delight, pursuit, poetry, desire. Romance isn't the icing on marriage; it's part of God's design. Married people should still woo each other.",q:["How were you pursued during dating that you'd love to experience again?","What makes each of you feel desired — words, touch, surprises, effort?","What has crowded out romance — and is any of it actually more important?","What's the most romantic thing your spouse has done? Retell it.","'Come away with me' — where would you go together if logistics vanished?"],act:"Plan and execute one intentional act of pursuit this week — a real date, a love note, a surprise. Effort speaks.",prayer:"Father, You invented romance. Keep us pursuing each other all our lives."},
{t:"A Time for Everything",ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:1-8",intro:"'For everything there is a season.' Marriages move through seasons — newlywed spring, child-raising summer, career winters, griefs and harvests — and each demands something different. Trouble comes when we judge one season by another's rules. Naming your current season together brings patience and perspective.",q:["What season is your marriage in right now? Name it honestly.","What does this season demand that previous ones didn't?","What from a past season do you miss? What are you glad has passed?","What is this season teaching you that no other could?","How can you be teammates in this season instead of critics of it?"],act:"Write down together: 'We are in a season of ____. In it we will hold onto ____ and let go of ____.' Put it somewhere visible.",prayer:"Lord of every season, give us grace for this one — and hope for the next."},
{t:"Trials as Teammates",ref:"James 1:2-4",intro:"'Count it all joy when you fall into various trials, knowing that the testing of your faith produces endurance.' Trials either drive couples apart or weld them together — the difference is whether you face them side-by-side or across from each other. The trial is never the enemy; it's the anvil.",q:["What trial has most tested your marriage so far? What did it produce?","Under pressure, do you turn toward each other or into yourselves?","How can suffering possibly connect to 'joy' in James's math?","What current pressure needs to be relabeled 'ours' instead of 'mine' or 'yours'?","What has endurance already built in your marriage that ease never could?"],act:"Identify your current biggest pressure. Write it on paper, set it in front of you both, and strategize against it — the problem on one side, the two of you together on the other.",prayer:"Father, weld us together in every trial. Let endurance finish its work in us."},
{t:"Hope That Doesn't Disappoint",ref:"Romans 5:3-5",intro:"Paul traces an unlikely chain: suffering produces perseverance, perseverance character, character hope — and hope doesn't disappoint, because God's love has been poured into our hearts. Couples need shared hope like lungs need air. This week, check your hope supply.",q:["What are you genuinely hopeful about right now — individually and as a couple?","Where has hope worn thin? What wore it down?","Looking back, what past suffering actually produced character in your marriage?","How does God's love 'poured into our hearts' refill hope when circumstances don't?","What hope do you want to hand your kids or the people watching your marriage?"],act:"Each finish this sentence aloud: 'A year from now, I hope we...' Then pray over both answers together.",prayer:"God of hope, refill us. Let our marriage run on hope that doesn't disappoint."},
{t:"Anxious for Nothing, Together",ref:"Philippians 4:6-7",intro:"'In nothing be anxious, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God.' Anxiety in one spouse always leaks into the marriage; peace, prayed for together, guards both hearts. You can't always fix each other's worries — but you can always carry them to God together.",q:["What is each of you most anxious about right now?","How does your anxiety show up at home — irritability, withdrawal, control?","What's the difference between your spouse fixing your worry and joining you in it?","Have you ever prayed together about a worry and felt the 'peace that passes understanding'?","What worry needs to move from your head to a shared prayer this week?"],act:"Make a two-column list: 'What's worrying us' and 'What we're thankful for.' Pray through both columns together — petitions with thanksgiving, exactly as the verse says.",prayer:"Father, we hand You our anxieties together. Guard both our hearts with Your peace."},
{t:"Commit Your Plans",ref:"Proverbs 16:3,9",intro:"'Commit your deeds to the LORD, and your plans shall succeed... A man's heart plans his course, but the LORD directs his steps.' Couples make hundreds of decisions — jobs, homes, schools, budgets. The question isn't whether to plan, but whether God gets the blueprints early or a courtesy copy later.",q:["How do you two currently make big decisions? What works and what doesn't?","When has God redirected a plan you'd made — and how did it turn out?","Do you genuinely seek God's direction together, or decide first and pray after?","Is there a decision on the table right now you haven't truly prayed about together?","What makes it hard when one of you feels peace about a direction and the other doesn't?"],act:"Take your biggest pending decision and commit it to God together this week: pray about it three separate times before discussing it further.",prayer:"Lord, we hand You our plans while they're still drafts. Direct our steps."},
{t:"Generous Together",ref:"2 Corinthians 9:6-8",intro:"'He who sows bountifully will also reap bountifully... God loves a cheerful giver.' Generosity is one of the most bonding things a couple can practice — giving together aims both hearts outward at the same target. And God's promise stands behind it: He supplies the seed for sowers.",q:["What's the most joyful giving you've ever done as a couple?","Do you two agree on generosity — how much, to whom, how often?","What makes giving feel 'cheerful' versus pressured for each of you?","Who in your life right now has a need you could quietly meet?","What would it look like to sow more 'bountifully' — money, time, hospitality?"],act:"Choose one generous act to do together this week — anonymous if possible. Notice what it does to your sense of partnership.",prayer:"Father, You gave first and gave most. Make us cheerful, united givers."},
{t:"An Open Home",ref:"1 Peter 4:8-9",intro:"'Be hospitable to one another without grumbling' — and Peter ties it to fervent love that covers a multitude of sins. Hospitality isn't entertainment or a spotless house; it's making people feel that there's room for them in your life. Couples who welcome others together often find their own bond strengthened.",q:["Who has made you feel deeply welcome? What did they actually do?","What stops you from opening your home more — mess, cost, energy, schedule?","How do your hospitality styles differ, and how can they complement?","Who is lonely in your circle right now — someone a simple dinner would bless?","'Without grumbling' — what would make hosting feel joyful instead of stressful?"],act:"Invite someone — a person, couple, or family — for a simple meal in the next two weeks. Simple counts: pizza and paper plates qualify.",prayer:"Lord, make our home an outpost of Your welcome."},
{t:"Children: A Heritage",ref:"Psalm 127",intro:"'Unless the LORD builds the house, they labor in vain who build it... children are a heritage of the LORD.' This psalm holds two truths together: children are a gift, and anxious toil without God is vanity. Whether you're raising kids now or shaping the next generation another way, the building crew matters more than the effort level.",q:["Where are you 'laboring anxiously' as parents (or in your family role) instead of trusting the Builder?","What does it change to see children as a 'heritage' — a trust from God, not a possession?","What are you most intentionally trying to build into your kids or the young people in your life?","Verse 2 says God 'gives sleep to his loved ones.' Where do you need to rest instead of strive?","Are you two aligned on parenting? Where do you pull in different directions?"],act:"Have a 30-minute 'builders meeting': what are we building into our kids this year, and what's one anxious toil we can hand back to God?",prayer:"Lord, build our house — we don't want to labor in vain. Thank You for the heritage You've given us."},
{t:"Faith Taught at Home",ref:"Deuteronomy 6:5-7",intro:"Love God with all your heart — then 'teach these words diligently to your children, and talk of them when you sit in your house, when you walk by the way, when you lie down, and when you rise up.' Faith transfers less through lectures and more through atmosphere: God woven into ordinary moments.",q:["How was faith modeled (or not) in your childhood homes?","What do your kids or others actually see of your faith day to day?","Which ordinary moments — meals, drives, bedtimes — could carry more of God naturally?","What questions about God have the kids in your life asked lately?","Verse 5 puts loving God first — before teaching. Why does that order matter?"],act:"Pick one daily 'doorpost moment' (a meal, a drive, bedtime) and add one small God-conversation to it every day this week. Your family's morning study counts — keep it going.",prayer:"Father, let our home breathe faith. Teach us first, so we can teach diligently."},
{t:"Content in Every Season",ref:"Philippians 4:11-13",intro:"'I have learned in whatever state I am, to be content in it.' Contentment is learned, Paul says — twice. Discontent in a marriage is corrosive: it whispers that the grass, the house, the income, even the spouse could be upgraded. Contentment isn't settling; it's receiving what God gave with both hands.",q:["Where does discontent creep into your thinking — house, money, stage of life, each other?","What did you once long for that you now have — and have stopped celebrating?","How does comparison (neighbors, social media) feed discontent in your home?","What's the difference between godly ambition and restless discontent?","What would it look like to fully 'move in' to the life you actually have?"],act:"Take a slow tour of your actual life together — home, photos, people — and out loud name what you'd once have prayed desperately to receive. Thank God for each one.",prayer:"Lord, teach us contentment. We receive this life — and each other — with open hands."},
{t:"Run Your Own Race",ref:"Galatians 6:4-5",intro:"'Let each man examine his own work... For each man will bear his own load.' Comparison is a thief in marriage: comparing your spouse to others, your marriage to highlight reels, your life to imagined alternatives. Paul's remedy is to test your own work — run your race, build your marriage, and let its joy be its own.",q:["Where does comparison sneak in — other couples, other lifestyles, social media?","Have either of you felt compared by the other, even subtly? What did it feel like?","What is genuinely, uniquely good about your marriage that no comparison captures?","What 'load' has God given your family that's yours alone to carry — and how are you doing?","How would this season feel if comparison were completely turned off?"],act:"Do a comparison fast this week: when you catch yourself comparing (spouse, house, life), immediately name one thing you're grateful for in what you actually have.",prayer:"Father, free us from measuring our life against anyone else's. Help us run our own race with joy."},
{t:"Worth More Than Rubies",ref:"Proverbs 31:10-12, 25-29",intro:"The excellent partner in Proverbs 31 is praised for character — strength, dignity, wisdom, faithfulness — and verse 28 shows the response: her husband praises her. The passage isn't just a portrait of a great spouse; it's a picture of a spouse who is seen and celebrated out loud. Praise is part of the design.",q:["Read the passage. What qualities in it do you see in your spouse? Say them out loud.","'Her husband praises her' — do you praise each other enough? In front of others?","What strength does your spouse carry that the world doesn't see?","'She can laugh at the days to come' — what gives a person that kind of confidence?","How does being trusted (v.11) empower a spouse to flourish?"],act:"Write your spouse a short 'Proverbs 31-style' tribute — five specific lines of praise — and read it to them face to face.",prayer:"Father, thank You for a spouse worth more than rubies. Teach us to praise each other out loud."},
{t:"Love That Lays Itself Down",ref:"Ephesians 5:25-28",intro:"'Husbands, love your wives, even as Christ also loved the assembly and gave himself up for it.' The measuring stick for married love is the cross — love that spends itself for the other's flourishing. This is love as sacrifice: not grand gestures mainly, but the daily dying of preferences, comfort, and self-interest.",q:["Where has your spouse sacrificed for you in ways you may have under-acknowledged?","What daily 'small dying' — comfort, preference, time — does love currently ask of each of you?","Christ's love makes His bride flourish ('holy... in splendor'). Is your love making your spouse flourish?","What's the difference between sacrifice that's chosen and martyrdom that keeps score?","Where is one of you being asked to lay something down in this season?"],act:"Each identify one preference to lay down this week for the other's flourishing — and do it without announcing the score.",prayer:"Jesus, You gave Yourself up for us. Teach us to love each other with laid-down love."},
{t:"Out of Reverence for Christ",ref:"Ephesians 5:21,33",intro:"Before any specific instruction to husbands or wives, Paul writes: 'Subject yourselves to one another in the fear of Christ.' Mutual deference — each putting the other's good above their own winning — is the soil the whole passage grows in. And verse 33 lands on the pair every marriage needs: love and respect.",q:["What does 'submitting to one another' look like in a healthy, practical sense?","Which do you each feel more starved for — love or respect? How can you tell?","How do you show respect when you disagree with each other's decisions?","Where do power struggles show up in your marriage — money, parenting, schedules?","How does doing this 'out of reverence for Christ' change the motivation entirely?"],act:"Each ask the other: 'What's one thing I do that makes you feel respected — and one thing that doesn't?' Just listen. Adjust one behavior this week.",prayer:"Lord, out of reverence for You, teach us to honor and defer to each other."},
{t:"First, Be Reconciled",ref:"Matthew 5:23-24",intro:"Jesus says if you're at the altar and remember your brother has something against you — leave the gift, go make it right, then come back. Reconciliation outranks religious activity. In marriage, that means a genuine apology is more urgent than looking fine at church. The order of operations is striking: peace with each other, then worship.",q:["What makes a real apology different from 'I'm sorry you feel that way'?","Which of you finds apologizing harder, and why?","Is there anything unresolved between you that you've been worshipping 'around'?","Have you ever felt the difference in worship when things were right between you versus not?","What does making it right require beyond words — changed behavior, restitution, time?"],act:"Learn the full apology together: 'I was wrong when I ___. It hurt you by ___. Will you forgive me?' Each use it once this week for something real, however small.",prayer:"Jesus, keep our accounts short. Make us reconcilers before we are performers."},
{t:"Love Covers",ref:"1 Peter 4:8",intro:"'Above all things be earnest in your love among yourselves, for love covers a multitude of sins.' Every marriage generates a multitude of small offenses — forgotten tasks, sharp tones, thoughtless moments. Love's daily work is coverage: choosing not to convert every irritation into a case file. Cover isn't denial; it's grace deciding what's worth a conversation and releasing the rest.",q:["What small offenses do you tend to collect instead of cover?","What's the difference between covering a sin and burying a real issue?","When has your spouse graciously covered one of your worst moments?","'Earnest' love — stretched-out love — costs something. What does it cost you?","What's one recurring irritation you could simply decide to cover with grace?"],act:"This week, when a minor irritation flares, silently pray 'love covers it' and let it go. Save conversations for what truly matters — and notice how few things do.",prayer:"Father, Your love covered all our sin. Give us earnest love that covers each other's rough edges."},
{t:"A Heart at Peace",ref:"Proverbs 14:30",intro:"'The life of the body is a heart at peace, but envy rots the bones.' Insecurity and jealousy — of attention, success, even each other's ease — quietly rot a marriage from the inside. The antidote is a heart at peace: secure in God's love, secure in each other's commitment, glad for each other's wins.",q:["Do you ever feel envious of your spouse — their freedom, success, friendships, ease? Be honest.","What makes each of you feel insecure in the relationship, even irrationally?","How do you celebrate each other's wins? Do either of you compete instead?","What reassurance actually settles your heart when insecurity rises?","What would a 'heart at peace' change about how you handle time apart?"],act:"Each share one insecurity you carry in the relationship, and let your spouse speak specific reassurance over it. Then celebrate one recent win of your spouse's as if it were your own — because it is.",prayer:"Lord, root out envy and insecurity. Give us hearts at peace with You and each other."},
{t:"A Cheerful Heart Is Medicine",ref:"Proverbs 17:22",intro:"'A cheerful heart makes good medicine, but a crushed spirit dries up the bones.' Laughter is a marriage's vitamin: couples who laugh together weather more and resent less. Somewhere along the way, many marriages get grimly efficient. This week's assignment is joy — on purpose.",q:["What made you laugh hardest together? Tell the story again, badly if necessary.","Has your marriage gotten too serious? What made it that way?","What does your spouse do that still genuinely delights you?","What did you do for fun in year one that you haven't done in years?","What 'dries up your bones' fastest — and what's the cheerful antidote?"],act:"Engineer laughter this week: a funny movie, a ridiculous game, retelling your worst-date stories. Grim efficiency is banned for one evening.",prayer:"Father, restore our laughter. Give us cheerful hearts as medicine for whatever ails us."},
{t:"Magnify the Lord with Me",ref:"Psalm 34:1-3",intro:"'Oh magnify the LORD with me. Let us exalt his name together.' Worship was designed to be shared — and something unique happens when a couple exalts God together: two hearts aimed at the same throne end up nearer each other. Shared worship is intimacy of the deepest kind.",q:["What's your most powerful memory of worshipping together?","Does worshipping side by side feel natural or awkward to you? Why?","What does 'magnifying' God mean — making Him bigger, or seeing Him truer?","How could worship enter your week beyond Sunday — music in the car, kitchen, evenings?","What has God done for your family lately that deserves exalting 'together'?"],act:"Have one intentional worship moment together this week — play a favorite worship song and actually stop to sing or pray through it, just the two of you.",prayer:"Lord, we magnify You together. Draw us closer to each other as we draw near to You."},
{t:"A Ministry Team",ref:"Acts 18:24-26",intro:"Priscilla and Aquila appear in Scripture always as a pair — tentmakers, church hosts, and here, gently mentoring the brilliant Apollos together. God loves deploying couples. Your marriage isn't just for you; it's a ministry unit with a combined skill set no one else has.",q:["What has God uniquely equipped you two to do together — hospitality, teaching, helping, building?","When have you served together and felt that 'this is what we're for' feeling?","Who are your 'Apollos' — people a step behind you that you could quietly invest in?","What keeps you serving separately (or not at all) instead of together?","If your marriage had a mission statement, what would it be?"],act:"Draft a one-sentence mission statement for your marriage this week. Then pick one act of service to do together this month that fits it.",prayer:"Lord, make our marriage a ministry team. Show us the work You prepared for us together."},
{t:"Pray for One Another",ref:"James 5:16",intro:"'Confess your sins to one another and pray for one another, that you may be healed.' Praying for your spouse changes them — and changes you. It's nearly impossible to stay critical of someone you're faithfully lifting before God. Confession plus intercession is James's prescription for healing.",q:["What do you each currently pray for your spouse — specifically?","What would you love your spouse to be praying for you?","Confessing to each other requires safety. How safe is confession in your marriage?","When has prayer visibly changed something in your spouse — or in your heart toward them?","What healing — of habit, hurt, or health — should you be praying for together?"],act:"Exchange prayer lists: each write three specific things you want prayer for. Pray for your spouse's list daily this week.",prayer:"Father, make us faithful intercessors for each other. Heal what needs healing between us."},
{t:"Many Plans, One Purpose",ref:"Proverbs 19:21",intro:"'There are many plans in a man's heart, but the counsel of the LORD will prevail.' Couples need shared dreams — and open hands. Talking about the future together (the five-year picture, the someday-list) is bonding; holding it before God keeps it from becoming an idol or a battleground.",q:["What's on each of your 'someday' lists that you've never said out loud?","Where do your dreams align beautifully? Where do they tug in different directions?","Has God ever overruled one of your plans for something better?","What dream have you deferred so long it's gone quiet? Should it be revived?","How do you hold plans firmly enough to pursue and loosely enough to surrender?"],act:"Have a dream session: each share your honest five-year picture. Find the overlaps, note the differences without judging, and pray the whole thing into God's hands.",prayer:"Lord, we dream with open hands. Let Your counsel prevail over all our plans."},
{t:"Even to Gray Hairs",ref:"Isaiah 46:4",intro:"'Even to old age I am he, and even to gray hairs I will carry you.' God's commitment to His people spans every stage — and marriage is designed for the same arc. You are not just spouses for this season; you're building the couple you'll be at seventy. Every habit today is a brick in that house.",q:["Picture yourselves at seventy: what do you hope is true of your marriage then?","What couples ahead of you do you want to be like? What are they doing right?","What current habit, unchanged, will you be glad of in thirty years? Which one will you regret?","How does God's 'I will carry you' promise steady you about aging, health, and change?","What do you want to still be doing together at every age?"],act:"Write a short letter to 'us at seventy' together — what you're building for them now — and seal it somewhere you'll find it again.",prayer:"Faithful God, You will carry us to gray hairs. Build in us a marriage that lasts the whole road."},
{t:"A Legacy of Faith",ref:"2 Timothy 1:5",intro:"Paul traces Timothy's sincere faith back through his grandmother Lois and mother Eunice — faith handed down like an heirloom. Your marriage is writing a letter to generations you may never meet. The question isn't whether you'll leave a legacy; it's what it will say.",q:["What faith heirlooms — habits, prayers, convictions — were handed down to each of you?","What from your family lines do you want to pass on? What do you want to end with you?","What will your kids (or those watching you) say your marriage taught them about God?","What ordinary practice now could become a Lois-and-Eunice legacy later?","If your great-grandchildren heard one sentence about your marriage, what should it be?"],act:"Start one legacy artifact this week: a family prayer journal, a recorded story, a written blessing — something faith-shaped that outlasts you.",prayer:"Father, let sincere faith run through our family line. Make our marriage an heirloom of grace."},
{t:"Live in Harmony",ref:"Romans 12:16-18",intro:"'Be of the same mind one toward another... If it is possible, as much as it is up to you, be at peace with all men.' Disagreement is inevitable; disharmony is optional. Paul's phrase 'as much as it is up to you' puts the responsibility where it belongs — on your own contribution to peace, which is the only part you control.",q:["What do you currently disagree about that keeps resurfacing?","Can you disagree and still be 'of the same mind' — same team, same direction? How?","What's 'up to you' in your recurring conflicts — what's your honest contribution?","When has one of you kept peace by absorbing something rather than escalating? Was it healthy?","What would harmony (not just ceasefire) look like on your most-argued topic?"],act:"Take your most recurring disagreement and each write down: 'My contribution to this conflict is ___.' Share papers. Discuss only your own part, not theirs.",prayer:"Lord, as far as it depends on each of us, make us peacemakers in our own home."},
{t:"Kindness Around Your Neck",ref:"Proverbs 3:3-4",intro:"'Don't let kindness and truth forsake you. Bind them around your neck; write them on the tablet of your heart.' Kindness in marriage isn't a mood — it's a practiced habit worn daily like a necklace. Researchers say kindness is the single greatest predictor of marital satisfaction. Solomon said it first.",q:["What's the kindest thing your spouse does regularly?","Where has kindness eroded into efficiency, sarcasm, or autopilot?","Why is it often easier to be kind to strangers than to your spouse?","What does 'binding' kindness — making it non-optional — look like practically?","What small kindness, done daily, would most change the temperature of your home?"],act:"Choose one daily kindness ritual to 'bind on' this week — coffee made, a text at noon, a real greeting at the door — and keep it all seven days.",prayer:"Father, bind kindness and truth around our necks. Write them on our hearts toward each other."},
{t:"One Flesh",ref:"1 Corinthians 7:3-5",intro:"God designed marital intimacy — 'one flesh' is His phrase from the very beginning (Genesis 2:24), His idea, His gift. Paul is remarkably practical: spouses belong to each other, and deprivation (except for agreed seasons of prayer) opens a door to temptation. Intimacy thrives on the same things the rest of marriage does: honesty, generosity, and unhurried attention.",q:["Is intimacy a comfortable topic between you, or avoided? Why?","What helps each of you feel close and connected — and have you actually said it out loud?","What crowds intimacy out in this season — exhaustion, schedules, unresolved conflict, screens?","Paul frames intimacy as mutual self-giving, not demand. What does that change?","What's one honest need or hope in this area you've never voiced?"],act:"Have an unhurried, phone-free evening together this week. Talk honestly about this area of your marriage — gently, generously, without criticism.",prayer:"Father, thank You for the gift of oneness. Keep us generous, honest, and tender with each other."},
{t:"As for Our House",ref:"Joshua 24:15",intro:"'Choose you this day whom you will serve... but as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD.' Joshua's declaration is a household decision, made out loud, in public, on purpose. Every home serves something — comfort, achievement, chaos, Christ. This week, you decide again, together, and say so.",q:["If someone audited your family's time and money, what would they conclude your house serves?","What 'gods of the land' — culture's defaults — quietly compete for your household?","What did each of you inherit in the 'whom we serve' department, for better or worse?","What does serving the Lord as a household actually involve this year?","Why does declaring it out loud — like Joshua — matter more than assuming it?"],act:"Write your own household declaration together — one or two sentences beginning 'As for our house...' Post it where your family will see it.",prayer:"Lord, we choose You again today. As for us and our house, we will serve the LORD."},
{t:"Love Never Fails",ref:"1 Corinthians 13:8,13",intro:"'Love never fails... Now abide faith, hope, and love — these three; and the greatest of these is love.' Everything else runs out: prophecies cease, knowledge passes, seasons end, bodies age. Love is the thing that survives. A year of studying marriage lands here: the greatest of these — the thing most worth building — is love.",q:["Looking back over this year of studies, what conversation changed you most?","Where has your love visibly grown in the past year? Be specific.","'Love never fails' — what has your love outlasted so far?","Faith, hope, and love: which is strongest in your marriage right now? Which needs feeding?","What do you want to be true of your love one year from today?"],act:"Celebrate: go somewhere meaningful to you both and toast the year. Each share three ways you've seen love grow — and one commitment for the year ahead.",prayer:"Father, You are love, and Your love never fails. Make ours the same — for all the years You give us."}
];
